{
  "image_name": "4b72b1be0a4ced092aa08f92c5eef7d5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how an atherosclerotic plaque forms and becomes unstable inside an artery wall. Lipoproteins such as LDL, VLDL, and Lp(a) move from the arterial lumen into the intima, where they are trapped by proteoglycans and then become oxidized. This appears to activate the endothelium, with adhesion molecules like ICAM1 and VCAM1 helping circulating monocytes attach and migrate into the vessel wall. These monocytes differentiate into macrophages, which phagocytose the retained lipids and turn into foam cells.\n\nAs lipid uptake continues, macrophages become activated and release inflammatory signals, including cytokines and microRNAs, amplifying local inflammation. The plaque then enlarges as foam cells accumulate and die. Defective autophagy and cell death contribute to a growing necrotic core filled with debris and cholesterol crystals. The advanced lesion is shown with a thin fibrous cap overlying this core, suggesting vulnerability. Collagen in the cap is degraded by MMPs, and apoptotic cells are present near the surface, further weakening the structure.\n\nOverall, the image shows the progression from early lipid retention to chronic inflammation and formation of a dangerous, rupture-prone atherosclerotic plaque.",
